Body:
**Update from the author — the fix described above is wrong (or at least, not the right layer)**

After living with the copilot SDK namespace fix for a while, my agent and I realized we were patching the wrong layer. The "Minimal fix" section above changes vendored code inside the bundled copilot Responses SDK — it works, but it's fragile and misidentifies the root problem.

**What we actually ship now**: a ~10-line bun patch on `@ai-sdk/azure` itself, plus two small routing changes in `provider.ts`. Zero modifications to the copilot SDK.

### The `@ai-sdk/azure` patch (`patches/@ai-sdk%2Fazure@3.0.49.patch`)

```diff
--- a/dist/index.js
+++ b/dist/index.js
@@ -69,10 +69,14 @@
     let fullUrl;
     if (options.useDeploymentBasedUrls) {
       fullUrl = new URL(`${baseUrlPrefix}/deployments/${modelId}${path}`);
+    } else if (options.baseURL) {
+      fullUrl = new URL(`${baseUrlPrefix}${path}`);
     } else {
       fullUrl = new URL(`${baseUrlPrefix}/v1${path}`);
     }
-    fullUrl.searchParams.set("api-version", apiVersion);
+    if (!options.baseURL) {
+      fullUrl.searchParams.set("api-version", apiVersion);
+    }
     return fullUrl.toString();
```
*(Same change in `dist/index.mjs`.)*

This teaches `@ai-sdk/azure` to respect a custom `baseURL` without forcing deployment-based URL paths or appending `?api-version=`. Our Azure API gateway (and likely others like LiteLLM, Azure API Management, or Cloudflare AI Gateway) handles versioning and routing itself.

### The `provider.ts` routing change

```typescript
// Route Azure providers (and any useResponses: true) through sdk.responses()
// instead of sdk.languageModel() — this uses the native Responses API path
// rather than falling through to the bundled copilot SDK
: (model.providerID.includes("azure") || provider.options?.["useResponses"] === true) 
    && sdk.responses !== undefined
  ? sdk.responses(model.api.id)
  : sdk.languageModel(model.api.id)
```

With this routing, Azure models go through `@ai-sdk/azure`'s own `.responses()` method — which handles the `"openai"` namespace correctly — instead of being routed through the bundled copilot SDK (which uses `"copilot"`).

### Note on AI SDK v6

In v6, `@ai-sdk/azure` changed its providerOptions key from `"openai"` to `"azure"` ([migration guide](https://ai-sdk.dev/docs/migration-guides/migration-guide-6-0#responses-api-providermetadata-and-provideroptions-key)). The original fix above (`"copilot"` → `"openai"`) would be **wrong** in v6. Using the native `.responses()` path means the SDK handles its own namespace — we don't have to.

### Tracking the permanent fix

Our `@ai-sdk/azure` patch is a workaround until the SDK supports custom `baseURL` natively:

- **Upstream**: [vercel/ai #13956](https://github.com/vercel/ai/issues/13956) — `@ai-sdk/azure: baseURL mode appends /v1 and api-version — breaks custom gateways` (open, unassigned)
- **Related**: [anomalyco/opencode #18089](https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/issues/18089) — `@ai-sdk/openai headers not forwarded to HTTP requests when using Responses API with APIM` (open)

Once vercel/ai #13956 lands, the bun patch goes away and only the `provider.ts` routing change remains.

### Who this applies to

Anyone routing `@ai-sdk/azure` through a non-standard base URL (API gateways, proxies, APIM) who hits the `rs_` / reasoning item errors. The copilot SDK fix from the original issue still works for `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible` with `useResponses: true` on AI SDK v5, but if you're using `@ai-sdk/azure` directly, this is the cleaner path.

I should have caught this before filing. The copilot SDK analysis was correct but led to the wrong fix location. Leaving the original issue body intact for reference.

